Compiles. Now write tests. First test resources application.yaml, then:

1. `SwitchConditionEvaluatorTest` — pure unit test.
2. `WellFlowIntegrationTest` — @SpringBootTest + MockMvc.
3. `ConcurrencyIntegrationTest` — service-level concurrency.

Test application.yaml (full replacement):

```yaml
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:h2:mem:geothermal_test;MODE=MySQL
    username: sa
    password: ""
    driver-class-name: org.h2.Driver
    hikari:
      maximum-pool-size: 5
      minimum-idle: 1
      connection-timeout: 10000
      validation-timeout: 3000
  sql:
    init:
      mode: always
      encoding: UTF-8
  jackson:
    time-zone: UTC

server:
  port: 0
  shutdown: graceful

management:
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health

app:
  seed-enabled: false
```

Note: no DB_CLOSE_DELAY → DB released when pool closes at context shutdown. Both test classes share one context (same config) — fine; @BeforeEach cleans tables.

Cleanup order: DELETE FROM switch_request_sample; switch_request; idempotency_record; pressure_sample; run_segment; well_lock.

Base test class with cleanup + clock setup? Two integration classes; make an abstract base `AbstractIntegrationTest` with @SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc, @MockitoBean Clock, cleanup, helper methods (postJson etc.). 

Clock mocking: @MockitoBean Clock clock. In @BeforeEach: reset and stub. Mockito.lenient() needed since some tests (e.g., pure evaluator) don't use clock — but that's a separate class. In integration classes, stubbing happens in beforeEach; some tests might not trigger clock usage... most do. Use lenient to be safe.

Helper to create well scenario:
```java
long createSegment(String well, String mode, String start) // via mockMvc post
```

Let me write helper methods returning parsed JSON (JsonNode via ObjectMapper).

Test list (WellFlowIntegrationTest):
1. happyPath_switchApprovedAndTimeline
2. outOfRangeReading_rejectedWith422 (12.1 MPa scenario — the spec's required case)
3. insufficientSamples_rejected
